    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm looking a bit deeper into the specific kinetics of the rearrangement. Can you expand on the Amadori rearrangement specifically? I'm trying to understand the transition from the N-substituted glycosylamine to the ketoamine, and I'd also like to know if there are specific amino acids that tend to react more aggressively than others in this process.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Amadori rearrangement is a crucial intermediate in the Maillard reaction. It involves the conversion of an N-substituted glycosylamine (an aldehyde attached to an amine via a carbon) into a ketoamine (with a ketone). This transition is facilitated by the breakdown of the glycosylamine, which releases an amine that converts to a hemiacetal, leading to the creation of an unstable intermediate where an alpha-hydrogen atom on the adjacent carbon is abstracted, initiating the rearrangement to a ketoamine. As for specific amino acids, lysine often participates more readily due to its side chain, which can interact with the carbonyl group during this rearrangement, leading to a more pronounced color and flavor profile in certain meats or ingredients that are high in lysine residues. Understanding these dynamics can help elucidate the complexity of flavor compound evolution during the Maillard reaction.",
      "source": "target",
