      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that helps clarify the lysine part. One more thing for my section on practical applications: I'm looking at why certain proteins can sometimes react unpredictably or \"break down\" in ways that cause uneven browning or bitter notes even when the temperature is controlled. From a molecular standpoint, what causes those sudden, inconsistent shifts in the reaction pathway?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unpredictable protein degradation causing uneven browning or bitter notes can stem from several molecular anomalies. First, the unequal distribution of heat can cause localized overcooking, leading to thermal denaturation or fragmentation of proteins, which then react in a novel manner, often yielding undesirable flavors. Second, the presence of different amino acid residues can lead to varied response rates and product formation, as some react faster than others, causing imbalance. Third, the Maillard reaction is susceptible to fluctuations in the food matrix; areas with different pH values or moisture content can display distinct reactivity profiles. Finally, the presence of food additives, essential oils, or spices can interact with proteins, creating additional complex pathways that can promote off-flavors and uneven browning. It's important to consider these variables to present a comprehensive picture on protein behavior under cooking stress in your application.",
      "source": "target",
